{
  "dom_id": "step-q147887",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3},{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{1}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=\\frac{1}{5}, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0, & P({A}_{2})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{5}, & P({A}_{3})=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{4})=\\frac{2}{5}, & P({A}_{4})=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=4,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})) \\\\  & =1-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})+P(B|{A}_{4})P({A}_{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{3}{8})+(0)(\\frac{1}{4})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{8})+(\\frac{2}{5})(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{40})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
